A||N||9||DaveL||Lifters only?||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||05-08-2001||03:52 PM||||OK, 87 2.5 SE, A/T, and the 64 $ question is, is it worthwhile to replace lifters and pushrods on an engine with 100,000 miles, without going through the whole engine? <P>Lots of loud ticking noise lately, and looking at possible fixes short of complete overhaul. Budget as limited as my vocabulary...<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||05-08-2001||06:16 PM||||I would say, "Yes."<P>Except, I wouldn't replace a pushrod unless it's bent - or if an end were worn (I've never seen an obviously worn pushrod, BTW).  To check for a bent rod, roll it on a flat pane of glass - if it goes "tick, tick, tick," as it rolls, you know it's bent. <P>I'm guessing with eight lifters + a gasket kit, you might have around $100 in it.  That's <B>cheap</B>, compared to a car payment.  And you'll probably get <B>way</B> more than an extra month out of the car!<P>Another thing you'll get out of it, is a valuable learning experience, which will even be applicable to other cars in your future.<P>UPDATE /CORRECTION:<BR>Please keep in mind, theogre seems to know what he's talking about, <B>especially</B> when it comes to the Duke engines!  Since our responses differ, and if at this point you're uncertain which way to go, I humbly recommend that you give Ogre's response <B>much</B> more weight.<P>I only have one Duke-powered Fiero, it's a 1988 model, and I've hardly touched it. My response was based on my limited experiences with 2.8's, and with a 1982 Cavalier 1.8-liter "throwaway" car. <p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000002||theogre||05-08-2001||07:22 PM||||Why do you think you need to replace them? Nearly All 87's have noisy valves. Even when new. Try changing the oil. that often quiets them some.<P>In any case I would not put new lifters on an old cam unless it's been totally inspected. Other wise you could cimply chew up both the cam and the new lifters. Rollers aren't as bad about this as flats but I still wouln't do it.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||05-09-2001||07:53 AM||||The noise got so bad one morning that I thought a valve had lost a piece or something wild. I shut it immediately down and checked oil. It looked OK, so I restarted and drove to work with it making so much noise that people looked at me in traffic, and it wasn't because the car is so beautiful!<P>What damage could result from running with a bad lifter. Positive this is the problem, but I'm no expert texpert.||||reg||1
Z||000004||LivoniaFieroGT||05-09-2001||03:28 PM||||Had two partially collapsed lifters on mine for about a month.  Everytime the oil thinned they clattered loud.  Took the top off when I had the time and put new lifters and no pushrods into it.  I drove it for another year maybe 4k and sold it.  Seemed worth the $65 for the materials.  May still be out there running ....<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Cooter||05-09-2001||04:25 PM||||My Fiero buddy Bob has an 86 se with the noisy lifters. He has already had the head built, new valve springs and new lifters. It is still just as noisy as it was when he started. We have no idea what to do next, but he will probably end up with a 4.9 Caddy engine before much longer.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DaveL||05-10-2001||07:45 AM||||This engine is haunted!!! OK, I found 2 semi-loose plugs, which I replaced with AC/Delco's, changed the oil and filter, Valvoline 10W-30. Before I did this, I added a half quart of Dexron-3 to the crankcase and let it idle for a while.<P>Now the tick is changing, not nearly as loud, but still different than before. It stops completely when at idle. Maybe it'll just disappear.  Yeah, and I'm winning the lotto tonight too....<P>I have an idea that when doing an oil change, letting the crankcase drain for a longer period of time allows more dirt to escape.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||05-10-2001||03:09 PM||||I'm frequently surprised how often "basic" maintenance stuff can help (e.g. - change plugs, oil and filter, unless you did it last week).<P>As far as letting the oil drain a long time, FWIW, an oil change is sometimes an all-day job at my house.  Not that I'm working on it all day.  I'll drive up, shut the car off, put a pan under, pull the plug and filter, let the oil drain out for an hour while I do something else, and so forth.  My point is, I feel like draining as much dirty oil as possible doesn't hurt.       ||||reg||10
Z||000008||Jake_Dragon||05-10-2001||06:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaveL:<BR><B>changed the oil and filter, Valvoline 10W-30.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Try 40w or 50w oil.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://home1.gte.net/rebel33/sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-10-2001||07:15 PM||||I really would not recomend 50 weight.<P>I'm having nice luck with mobil 1 in mine. I did go to 10W-30 in it.<P>If you are sure you have a valve noise, first thing to do is make sure all the rocker nuts are tight.<P>Since you have to mess with it... Pull each P rod one at a time and make sure they aren't plugged with crud.<P>valve cover gaskets are cheap.... even if you have to mess with it more than once it's worth doing this.<P>Might want to do a compression test. Just to make sure you didn't burn a valve.<P>Also check the exhaust manifold.... they like to crack. I'm on my third one.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-10-2001).]||||reg||1
